Negative control for the ordering control's placement (G3 · E9 · AC5.1).

Restores the card-only sort: the display row withholds `SortControl` unless the
card view is on, so table view is back to "click a header or nothing" and the
ordering control is no longer part of the data surface's own line. Nothing else
moves — the control, its fields and its emit are untouched — so what goes red is
the one claim under test: the control is offered in BOTH views.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: display-row.spec.ts — the both-views cases (the ordering control renders
in table view, and writes the same criteria from either view).

diff --git a/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/runtime/components/DisplayRow.vue b/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/runtime/components/DisplayRow.vue
--- a/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/runtime/components/DisplayRow.vue
+++ b/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/runtime/components/DisplayRow.vue
@@ -18,7 +18,7 @@
            owns its ordering, so it is offered where the data is, whether or not
            there happen to be headers to click. -->
       <SortControl
-        v-if="hasSort"
+        v-if="hasSort && view === ListViewTypes.CARD"
         :fields="fields"
         :sort="sort"
         @update:sort="emit('update:sort', $event)"
